    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Japan’s modern history witnessed the increasing involvement of horses on the battlefield and in the colonies. The wars against Qing China and Tsarist Russia at the turn of the twentieth century made Japan realize the importance of horses in land-based warfare and its horse deficiency. The subsequent outbreak of World War I further revealed horses’ indispensable role in battlefield logistics, given their ability to adapt to varying terrains and their reliability in times of fuel shortages. The Japanese government and its army consequently carried out two horse improvement plans to transform its frail native horses into effective weapons, assistants, and tools. \r\nJapan’s occupation of Korea and Manchuria gave it access to the diverse and superior breeds of the East Asian continent. Its colonial governments and agencies eagerly crossbred local breeds with the improved Japanese horses to produce better hybrids, and developed horse racing industries in the colonies to finance their horse improvement projects. This thesis argues about Japan’s equine imperialism. It refers to the use of veterinary and eugenic methods to enhance the quality and quantity of horses for warfare and imperial expansion. Through hybridizing and producing horses, Japan had sufficient horse resources to help carry weapons and provisions to the front, make profits at racetracks, and plow newly acquired land for agrarian settlers, fueling the empire’s territorial and economic expansion. Horses were also subject to the empire’s biopolitics. Through crossbreeding, regulated mating, and stud castration, Japanese technocrats allowed hybridization between valuable strains while eliminating native breeds they deemed undesirable, transforming the equine landscape of East Asia.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
